From: Oliver Seiler <oseiler@unixg.ubc.ca>
Subject: Question: Seasonal Affective Disorder

All this talk about SAD has got me remembering about a drug called
Melatonin, which is a hormone produced by the pineal gland. Melatonin has
been linked to the biological circadian rhythms, being released at night
when the brain 'thinks' its sleeping. Levels generally decrease with age,
which is though to be one of the reasons why length and quality of sleep
generally decrease with age. Melatonin supplements have been tested on
such things as depression, jet lag, and SAD, with promising results (for
jet legs symptoms it works astonishingly well [Arendt et al. 1986]
[Claustrat, 1992])

One study with SAD and depression shows that nighttime release of
melatonin in patients is generally reduced, with abnormal sensitivity to
melatonin supression by light [Brown, 1989].

It is a non-prescription substance and is available from mail order
sources. Dosages can vary greatly between different people, but can be
anywhere from 0.1 to 200 mg (biigg range!) Starting at 3 mg at around 11pm
is probably a good place to start, tweaking for results. Supposedly timing
is especially important.

For more information, pick up a copy of 'Smart Drugs II' (Yes the sequel)
Lot's of useful information, with list of references for things discussed
as well.

Refs:

Arendt J, Aldhous M and Marks V.  Alleviation of jet-lag by melatonin:
   Preliminary results of controlled double-blind trial.
   Brit Med J 292: 1170, 1986.

Claustrat B, Brun J, David M, Sassolas G and Chazot G.  Melatonin and jet-lag:
   Confirmatory result using a simplified protocol.
   Biol Psychiatry (USA) 32(8): 705-11, 1992.

Brown GM. Psychoneuroendocrinology of depression.  Psychiatr J Univ Ott 14(2):
   344-8; Jun 1989.  Discussion follows article.

Hope this helps!

