>>puppet on a string.  A universe set up by a "God" or giant Mussolini.
>
>I dont understand why some people have to resort to mysticism (or quantum
>mechanics) whenever they find something they can't explain.

Quantum mechanics is not mysticism -- it's empirical fact.  It did sweep
away many cherished beliefs.

>Do you suggest that evolution is impossible in a strictly deterministic
>universe? Netiher natural selection nor mutation are random processes.

The part about "intelligent life" above wasn't an essential part of the
argument, but to understand how I arrived at this conclusion you'd have
to have read my post "QUANTUM: MMI vs. MWI".  Here's the reasoning:

I'm not saying that life couldn't evolve under strict determinism; only
that it would become uncompetitive.  In my ontology, our external world
consists of not one but an infinite (or very large) number of worlds;
all existing in a superposition.  If a proportion of these all include
a particular animal's brain at a specific place and time, and any 
physical differences in these brains are too small to result in a
separation of the individual's consciousness stream and mind, then the
individual's brain and mind can be considered as having extension in
"reality space" -- a _very_ large number of these parallel worlds.  For
a species to exist in a strictly deterministic universe each of its
members' brains would have to exist solely in a single universe.  But in
a single universe light loses its wave characteristic and becomes
merely a stream of photons which each extend for about 3 metres with
10nS duration.  There is no longer any way to discern colour. 

Even fish have cells in their visual systems that are consistent with
the opponent-process theory, in which the output of the cones is
received by cells that occur in sets of three kinds of opponent-process
pairs.  The sensory coding vector then translates to an experience in
colour qualia space.  But the successful species has to try to stay one
step ahead of its opponents, many of whom do have extension in reality
space.  So those who are limited to a single world are poorer at
identifying food and recognising danger, and the curtain falls....

> Determinism does not rule out free will, because complexity *can* (and
> does) arise from simplicity. Our free will is simply a consequence of
> evolution. It is nonsence to talk about free will at the physical (and
> maube biological) layer, because free will only relevant at the psycical
> level.

What people argue about is whether you can have free will at the psychical
/ psychological level if there is no freedom at the physical or biological
level.  My free will definition #1 is the simple "feeling of free will",
the most obvious form of "will".  Call this "external free will".  The
other question as to whether consciousness plays an active role or is 
just a by-product or even an illusion, is version #2; call this "internal
free will".  If you know that you've got external and internal free will,
and you're not subject to predestination, then it's great.  Life really
does have a purpose -- to select "good" futures rather than "bad", and
to try to become something far, far greater.  Genuine freedom!

> And please, give an example of an event that is completely independent of
> any previous event.

I gave an example of a tunneling electron.  An electron transition can
occur at any time -- no external agency determines precisely when.  The
best you can do is to predict how likely it will occur within a given
time.  It's not hard to imagine that a number of these "random" events
could "just happen" to occur in alignment with each other.  Radioactive
decay does not happen because something "caused" it -- it just happens
unpredictably.  Even a vacuum is full of a host of "virtual" particles
which keep appearing out of nowhere.  And our very universe(s) arose from
nowhere, unless it's someone else's basement universe.  About 10 ^ 68
joules of energy appeared from nothing in the first 10 ^ -32 seconds.

In the sort of free will that I'm claiming, we can never have total 
freedom.  Environment is one influence, and genetic predetermination is
another (more for some people than others).  But we can have sufficient
Executive control so as to provide for internal as well as external
freedom.

By the way, soon after I sent out the previous post about the relative
values of human beings, memes and genes, I decided on a little
modification for the "human" rating.  They still count as irreplaceable
and potentially priceless, but I'd add that a small minority have an
overall negative value.  Though there's no one on the Extropians list
that I'd place in that category!

