You seem to suggest that evolution has selected those species that had
extension in "reality space".  As an example, you give the selective advantage
of color discernment which you claim is better in an extended mind than in one
that is limited to a single deterministic universe.  However, your argument
about the properties of light in a single universe depends upon the many worlds
interpretation, and thus gives no support for that interpretation (except to
show that it may be self-consistent).  Don't get me wrong, it's an interesting
notion, I just don't find it necessary.  (Are you saying that if there's one
universe then it must be deterministic?  If so, why?)

I have a difficult time believing a theory which requires such postulates as
the existence of a vast number of superimposed parallel universes to explain
something like the paradoxes of quantum physics.  (A question, has the "EPR
Paradox", which you referred to as a thought experiment, been actually
tested?  Does it not require completely non-intrusive measurement methods?).

As to consciousness - the standard approach seems to be to define it (usually
introspectively) and to then proceed to look for it in computers or in quantum
physics.  How about instead let's study the systems where it arises?  Keep in
mind that modern science and mathematics know _very_ little about complex
dynamical systems, and that this is the reason life and the brain pose such a
puzzle.  (Let's look for the keys where we dropped them, not where the light is
- but I've bored you with all this before...)

