Richard Kennaway writes:
>Can anyone suggest a circuit for doing this?  I'm familiar with the basic
>dimmer circuit controlled by a mechanical potentiometer, and can easily
>make a circuit which will ramp a DC control voltage from 0 to peak in 20
>minutes, but cannot think of a simple way of using that signal to control a
>dimmer.  All I can think of is to replace the potentiometer with an
>analogue optocoupler.  Any electronic engineers here with suggestions for a
>voltage-controlled dimmer circuit?

If you can get hold of the Maplin catalogue, there's a couple of
interesting products, and it gives some useful circuit info.  In the 1993
edition (the 1994 one came out a couple months ago) there's a "Slow On/Slow
Off Dimmer" Velleman kit shown on p. 635.  (Priced at 16.95 pounds.)  The
dim on & off times are variable from 2 seconds to 1 hour, works on UK/USA
mains and controls 400W at 240V.  From the photo, it's got four ICs, about
25 resistors, 10 capacitors, 3 presets.  PCB is supplied, but no case.

On page 480 is the SLB0586, an "electronic dimmer" IC, price 4.65 pounds.
The circuit diagram shows pin 8 (trigger output) connected to the gate of
a C225M triac via a signal diode.  Parts needed are the IC, triac,
6 resistors, 5 capacitors, 4 diodes, a choke and a fuse.  Pin 5 (sensor
input) connects to a "sensor plate" via two 4M7 resistors.  Pin 3 
(integrator) connects to the IC's 0v pin via an RC, so maybe if the
resistor and capacitor were raised to give a period of 20 minutes this
might give the required effect just by pulsing the sensor input.
(There's no PCB supplied.)

