it was actually a rich boy's toy. A pistol sized crossbow. Because the
travel is so short, it requires a very stiff bow. It is cocked by turning a
giant wing nut. The threaded shaft running through the nut is attached at
the other end to the piece that anchors the bowstring, so as you turn the
nut the bowstring is pulled back. The thing works--I have fired it. He has
also made several very pretty crossbows of a more ordinary sort.

2. "Arbalests." I do not have my library with me here, but I belive the
term refers to two different things. One is an ordinary crossbow. The other
is a siege engine which looks like a crossbow but is actually powered by
twisted cords rather than a bent bow--the two skeins of cord are vertical,
each with a horizontal arm through it, giving an effect that looks like a
crossbow if you do not look carefully.

3. Trebuchets. I have known a number of people who have made trebuchets,
including two who made human powered ones. Instead of a counterweight you
have a lot of people pulling on ropes, trained to all pull at the same
time. I believe that is the earlier form of the machine. It works pretty
well.

When I was working on a counterweighted trebuchet, many years ago, the
problem we had was getting it to release at the right time. Angular
acceleration is positive until the counterweight reaches the bottom of its
arc, so the thing wants to throw horizontally--which does not give you very
good range. My guess is that one advantage of a trebuchet with a sling is
that it eliminates the requirement that the velocity of the projectile must
be at right angles to the rotating arm, and thus allows you to maximize
energy (by releasing with the counterweight all the way down) while still
releasing at the optimal angle for range (45 degrees).

Is this really an extropian topic?

