I'm interested in list and newsgroup mechanisms, in particular aiming
at improving S/N ratio, and my post should be viewed that context.
In particular, I'm not a party to Wiik vs May, and
as far as the outcome of this particular judgement is concerned, I'm
sure the principals can take care of themselves.

> From: Harry Shapiro <habs@panix.com>
> X-Message-Number: #93-10-600
> 
> 1) Any list member can file charges - they do not have to
> 	be party to the flame as everyone is effected by S/N
[... procedure points deleted ...]

My response concerns the way judgements are reported to the list:

I suggest that, instead of a succinct, "In John Smith vs Joe L. User, 
concerning message #73-13-1.23e23, I found in favor of John User", a
more detailed summary of the judgement be posted. I'm not advocating
thoroughly burying the moderator in paperwork, but just enough detail
to:

1) Describe the case, as a reminder to potentials offenders. If the
Rules aim at improving S/N ratio, then such reminders are useful.

2) Provide an easy way for the users to monitor the way the list is
being moderated. 

3) Avoid trivializing the procedure. If a case was important enough to
issue a judgement, maybe it's important enough to report to the public.

If paperwork becomes too time consuming, then the judge could choose
which cases he prosecutes, and simply ignore the others, according to
workload. That does not change the deterring effect of these procedures.
Considering how easy it is to file charges, as compared to resolving the
case, cases should rather be dropped rather than botched...

Warnings:
=========
The Rules mention that "warnings" are not issued anymore, but that does
not prevent individuals from issuing warnings to each other via private
email "I resented your message XXX-XX-XXX. Please refrain from flaming,
or I'll file charges. Potential sanctions include death by loss of list
privileges" :-) Are such private warnings in use? Does the judge consider
whether such warning was made and ignored when considering a case?

Judgement report:
=================
So, A judgement report could include:
- The one line summary,
- A summary of the complaint, 
- A copy of one of the offending messages (if applicable)
- An "opinion" detailing the reasoning behind the judgement (3-5 lines)
- A summary of the actions to be taken.


> 3) If you don't like a judgment, BUT are Not a party to it, feel
>       free to let me know but basically I don't "care." But don't
>       vent to the list.

"No flaming" is understood by most, but "no discussion" 8^?

The case of Wiik vs May shows some of the problems of strict anti-flaming
rules when even most everybody can be caught in that mood at some time or
other... I find that judgement difficult to relate to the Rules (although
I understand how it relates to their goal...) Does the list actually
evolve its rules as they are applied (case law and such)?  That would be
even more reason to document decisions...

